---
import ArticleLayout from "@layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="Flowforest website"
  description="The flowforest.com marketing site: a quiet, typographic home for local-first tools, built with Astro and deployed as static output."
  subtitle="Reduce the noise. Amplify the signal."
  eyebrow="2026 · Website / Flowforest"
  tags="Website · Astro · Typography · Static site"
>
  <figure slot="art" class="flowforest-cover">
    <a href="https://flowforest.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
      <img
        src="/assets/flowforest-website/cover.png"
        alt="The Flowforest homepage with a fine contour drawing across the page and the words Reduce the noise. Amplify the signal."
        width="1600"
        height="1000"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </a>
  </figure>

  <p class="lead">
    The public home for Flowforest, and the one place that has to explain what
    the practice is for. It sets out what the tools believe, then shows the
    three products without overselling any of them.
  </p>
  <a
    class="text-link"
    href="https://flowforest.com/"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noopener noreferrer"
  >
    Visit flowforest.com ↗
  </a>

  <section>
    <h2>An argument, not a feature list</h2>
    <p>
      The page leads with a position rather than a product:{" "}
      <em>Reduce the noise. Amplify the signal.</em> From there it moves through
      what Flowforest is, the three tools, and the principles behind them. Two
      sections do the heavier work. <em>What we believe</em> states the case for
      owning your own files, and <em>What we won’t build</em> rules out
      surveillance, attention extraction and lock-in. Stating the refusals
      openly is what makes the promises checkable.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>One typeface, one accent</h2>
    <p>
      The whole site runs on a white field, a near-black ink and a single warm
      grey. There is no colour palette, no card grid and almost no iconography.
      Hierarchy comes from type scale, letter spacing and line breaks, so the
      sentence breaks fall where they should read rather than where the column
      happens to end.
    </p>
    <p>
      The only ornament is the contour drawing behind the hero — fine dotted
      lines that drift across the page edges. It carries the name without
      illustrating it: forest, flow, and the slow work of tending both.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Showing the tools without screenshots</h2>
    <p>
      Each product gets a short entry: a number, a name, one sentence of
      purpose, three facts and a status. The visual beside it is drawn in HTML
      and CSS rather than captured from a device, so Flare, Runway and Slate
      stay consistent while the apps are still in development. Each one is
      marked plainly: Android, in development; desktop and web, in development;
      iPad and Mac, in development. Nothing claims a release date it cannot
      keep.
    </p>
    <figure class="flowforest-product">
      <img
        src="/assets/flowforest-website/products.png"
        alt="The Flare entry on the Flowforest site: a numbered label, the product name, a purpose line, three feature lines and an in-development status beside a phone drawn in CSS"
        width="1600"
        height="1234"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Flare, as the site presents it: purpose, facts and status in the same
        compact rhythm for each of the three tools.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How it is built</h2>
    <p>
      The site is Astro with static output. One layout holds the head, header
      and footer, and every style lives in a single global stylesheet. Assets
      are served from <code>public/</code> at the site root, so they never pass
      through the build. The only script is the hero canvas, loaded from the
      page rather than the frontmatter because frontmatter runs at build time,
      where there is no document to animate.
    </p>
    <p>
      Output is deployed to Cloudflare Pages as <code>dist/</code>, with
      security headers copied from <code>public/_headers</code>. Routes end in a
      slash, and the canonical tags match, so the privacy policy is served at
      <code>/flare/privacy-policy/</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <aside class="project-status">
    Live site · A marketing site for products still in development. It describes
    intended behaviour rather than shipped features.
  </aside>
  <a class="text-link" href="/journal/runway/">
    Read the Runway build note ↗
  </a>
</ArticleLayout>

<style>
  .flowforest-cover,
  .flowforest-product {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .flowforest-cover img,
  .flowforest-product img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
  .flowforest-product {
    margin-top: 32px;
  }
  figcaption {
    margin-top: 12px;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 10px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
</style>
